{"results":[{"url":"https://www.cantonrep.com/story/news/2020/10/09/joe-bidens-campaign-received-bat-light-boost-steelworkers/5931926002/","final_url":"https://www.cantonrep.com/story/news/2020/10/09/joe-bidens-campaign-received-bat-light-boost-steelworkers/5931926002/","title":"United Steelworkers use 'bat light' to promote Biden","description":"Steelworkers used projection images to promote Joe Biden presidential campaign in Canton on Thursday night.","language":"en","text":"NEWS\n\n# United Steelworkers use 'bat light' to promote Biden\n\nMalcolm Hall\n\nThe Repository\n\nUpdated Oct. 9, 2020, 8:21 a.m. ET\n\n**CANTON** Posted for about a half hour high on the exterior of a downtown 13-story office building was a written promotion for the presidential campaign of Joseph Biden and Kamala Harris.\n\n\"Biden-Harris\" was projected in light onto the building wall by an image projector placed on a sidewalk in the 100 block of Tuscarawas Street E.\n\nThe United Steelworkers brought their \"bat light\" promotion project to the Canton area Thursday as the labor union urged anyone passing by on the downtown streets to exercise their right to vote Nov. 3. And at the same time they were promoting the Democratic presidential ticket.\n\n\"Right now, that is the important message we want to get,\" said Amber Miller, a part of the United Steelworkers Political Action Committee. \"However, you choose to vote, make sure you are ready.\"\n\nThe United Steelworkers team made a handful of stops in the Canton area to project their promotional message. Other selected stops included the Pro Football Hall of Fame and the McKinley Monument.\n\n\"We looked where our (union) members were,\" said Miller, who is from the United Steelworkers' Pittsburgh office. \"We wouldn't go out in rural areas and do this. We are not going in areas where we know this message would not be very welcome. We are not here to antagonize.\"\n\nBiden, a former vice president, and Harris, a member of the U.S. Senate representing California, comprise the Democratic Party ticket seeking to oust Republican President Donald Trump and Vice President Mike Pence.\n\nTrump, during the 2016 election, won Stark County with 55.8% of the vote. His Democratic Party opponent, Hillary Clinton, captured 38.6% of the vote. Trump also won Ohio with 51.6% of the vote.\n\nThe United Steelworkers have been holding these image projection promotions in cities in various states of the Midwest.\n\n\"This week was kind of an Ohio week,\" Miller said.\n\nThree concerns the union is focusing on are affordable health care, retirement security and workers' rights on the job.\n\nMembers of the union said Biden provided satisfactory responses when these concerns were expressed to him. However, Trump reportedly failed to respond to the union.\n\n\"About a month ago, we started the project,\" said Jess Kamm Broomell, public relations officer for the United Steelworkers. \"We call it the bat light project. They are mostly get out the vote messages to remind people to get out and vote.\"\n\nUpon leaving the office building, the group's next stop was another downtown site, Arrowhead Vintage & Handmade Goods in the 500 block of Cleveland Avenue NW. The image promoting the Biden-Harris campaign was projected on the wall of the single-story building with a colorful mural in the background.\n\n\"This is great, this is awesome,\" said David Sherrill, owner of Arrowhead Vintage & Handmade Goods. \"My social media will see 5,000 to 6,000 people.\"\n\nWhile the promotional image was displayed on the downtown office building, it was visible from U.S. Route 30 on the south side of Canton, said Joe Plott, a unit chair of the United Steelworkers union representing rank-and-file employees at The Timken Co. bearing plant.\n\n\"I think it is encouraging because the Trump people have been so vocal,\" Plott said.\n\nMiller of the United Steelworkers clarified that these promotional messages were not supplied by the Biden campaign.\n\n\"We don't coordinate with them,\" Miller said. \"If we get some love from them, and they like what we did, yay! This is all something the Steelworkers came up with.\"\n\nReach Malcolm at 330-580-8305\n\nor malcolm.hall@cantonrep.com\n\nOn Twitter: mhallREP\n\nFeatured Weekly Ad","author":"Malcolm Hall","published_date":"2020-10-09T11:55:19Z","latency_ms":555.0563270007842,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}